  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1112 PM MDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nThe upper high that has been situated over the southern US the last \ncouple days will dissolve and redevelop itself over the desert SW \nWednesday. Meanwhile, a trough will swing by the northern portion of \nthe CONUS. We will continue to have above average moisture (PWs of \n1.2\"-1.4\") which will help fuel monsoonal thunderstorms tomorrow \nafternoon. A piece of energy from this trough looks to bring more \nareawide coverage for thunderstorms across the area. Upper high will \nstill be over the desert SW Thursday. A more average storm day is \nexpected Thursday with scattered to numerous storms in the area \nmountains and more isolated to scattered coverage in the lowlands. \nGusty outflow winds and moderate to heavy rainfall will continue \nto be the primary hazards each afternoon. By Friday, we'll still \nbe under the influence of a weakening ridge but we will be \nentraining drier, continental air leading to a downtick in storm \nactivity mostly focused out west where the best moisture will be. \nSomewhat better moisture pushes back into the area Saturday \nallowing for more typical monsoon thunderstorm activity, even \nheading into Sunday.",
